\description{
Uses \code{rstan} with pre-compiled Stan code, or \code{cmdstan}, to get posterior draws of parameters from a Bayesian ordinal (or binary) cumulative probability regression model, with a choice of 5 link families -- \code{borm()} is to \code{\link[=blrm]{blrm()}} as \code{\link[rms:orm]{rms::orm()}} is to \code{\link[rms:lrm]{rms::lrm()}}: it generalizes the single (logistic) link that \code{blrm()} implements to all of the link families \code{orm()} supports, and is designed, like \code{orm()}, to scale to a very large number of distinct Y values.  Unlike \code{blrm()}, \code{borm()} does not implement the partial proportional odds model (\code{ppo}/\code{cppo}) itself; \code{blrm()} remains the function to use for that. Instead \code{borm()} implements \code{ydep}, a more general Y-dependent effect generalization that plays \code{cppo}'s role: \code{yde} (paralleling \code{ppo}) identifies which predictors get a Y-dependent effect, and \code{ytrans} (paralleling \code{cppo}, but returning a matrix of one or more columns instead of a single score) specifies the shape(s) of that dependency. A single-column \code{ytrans} with a single \code{yde} predictor reduces mathematically to \code{blrm()}'s own \code{cppo} mechanism.
}
